{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.97,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U2", "U3", "U4", "J4"],
      "pins": ["U2.7", "U3.7", "U4.2", "U4.4", "J4.3"],
      "nets": ["SCK", "Net-(U4-Pad2)"],
      "observed": "J4.3 and U2.7 are on SCK, which is also driven by U4.4, a buffer output. U4.2 is driven by U3.7, but U3.7 is on a separate net, Net-(U4-Pad2). Thus U3's PB2 does not receive the programmer's SCK; instead, its signal is buffered onto SCK, where it can contend with U2.7 and an external programmer.",
      "expected": "For the AVR-ISP interface, the programmer's SCK should reach each target MCU's clock-input pin. The SCK net should not also be driven by a buffer output or another MCU output.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"12\") (name \"SCK\") ... (node (ref \"J4\") (pin \"3\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"7\") ... (node (ref \"U4\") (pin \"4\") (pintype \"output\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"15\") (name \"Net-(U4-Pad2)\") ... (node (ref \"U3\") (pin \"7\") (pinfunction \"PB2\")) (node (ref \"U4\") (pin \"2\") (pintype \"input\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"J4\") (value \"AVR-ISP-6\")"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The ISP clock path to U3 is not direct, so U3 may not be programmable through J4. The buffer output, U2's PB2 output, and an attached programmer can also drive SCK against one another, risking failed programming or electrical contention.",
      "confidence": 0.97
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U2", "U3"],
      "pins": ["U2.1", "U2.5", "U2.6", "U2.2", "U2.3", "U3.1", "U3.5", "U3.6", "U3.2", "U3.3"],
      "nets": ["RST", "SDA", "PWM", "POT", "TACH"],
      "observed": "Both powered AVR microcontrollers share reset and several control/interface nets: PB0 on SDA, PB1 on PWM, PB3 on POT, PB4 on TACH, and reset on RST. Neither is marked as an alternate or unpopulated device.",
      "expected": "A controller should avoid placing two independently executing MCUs on the same control nets unless their roles and output arbitration are explicitly defined; shared push-pull outputs can contend.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"10\") (name \"PWM\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"PB1\")) (node (ref \"U3\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"PB1\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"13\") (name \"SDA\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"AREF/PB0\")) (node (ref \"U3\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"PB0\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"11\") (name \"RST\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"~{RESET}/PB5\")) (node (ref \"U3\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"~{RESET}/PB5\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If both MCUs are populated and running firmware, their shared nets can have conflicting drive states, causing incorrect fan-control, tachometer, or interface behavior. The shared reset and ISP-related connections also prevent treating them as independent targets.",
      "confidence": 0.87
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The SCK/ISP wiring has a clear direction and contention problem: U3's PB2 is isolated from the programmer clock while a buffer output and U2's PB2 share SCK. The two powered AVRs also share multiple control nets, creating additional risk of conflicting outputs."
}

